<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
    <string>en</string>
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>Jarvis</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
    <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
    <string>6.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>

    <!--
      THE PERMISSION THIS APP LIVES OR DIES BY.

      Since iOS 14, an app cannot open a connection to a private address - or
      even discover that one exists - until the user has allowed it, and the
      prompt shows this exact sentence. If it is missing the app is killed at
      the first connection attempt, with a crash report that says nothing about
      why. It is worded to explain the whole point rather than to satisfy the
      requirement.
    -->
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Jarvis needs to reach your PC on this Wi-Fi network so it can show you the HUD, stream your screen and move your mouse and keyboard. Nothing leaves your network.</string>

    <!--
      Cleartext to the LAN, and to nothing else.

      There is no certificate authority that will issue a certificate for
      192.168.1.10, so https to a PC on a home network is not a thing that
      exists. NSAllowsLocalNetworking is Apple's narrow exception for exactly
      this case: it permits http:// and ws:// to link-local and private
      addresses while leaving App Transport Security fully in force for
      everything on the internet. NSAllowsArbitraryLoads - which is what most
      people reach for - would switch ATS off for the whole app, and is both
      unnecessary here and a reason to be rejected from the App Store.
    -->
    <!--
      THE MICROPHONE, FOR TALKING INTO THE ROOM THE PC IS IN.

      Only ever opened while the TALK button is being HELD - it is push to hold
      rather than a toggle precisely so that this cannot be left running in a
      pocket - and the session is deactivated the moment it is released, which
      is what takes the orange recording dot away again.
    -->
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Hold TALK and your voice comes out of your PC's speakers, so you can talk to whoever is in the room. The microphone is only on while you are holding the button.</string>

    <!--
      Cleartext to the LAN and to the tailnet, and to nothing else.

      NSAllowsLocalNetworking is Apple's narrow exception for a PC on a home
      network: there is no certificate authority that will issue a certificate
      for 192.168.1.226, so https to one is not a thing that exists. It covers
      RFC 1918 and link-local addresses and leaves App Transport Security fully
      in force for everything on the internet.

      What it does NOT cover is 100.64.0.0/10, which is where Tailscale puts
      every node - that is carrier-grade NAT space, not private space, so as far
      as ATS is concerned it is a public address being asked for over http. The
      raw socket is unaffected (ATS is a URL-loading-system policy and the
      connection is an NWConnection), but the HUD tab is a WKWebView and a web
      view goes through ATS like anything else. So the tailnet gets a named
      exception rather than a blanket one: ts.net is Tailscale's MagicDNS
      domain, netreach.py reports this machine's name under it, and
      reachable_addresses() puts that name AHEAD of the bare 100.x address for
      this exact reason.

      The name the app actually tries FIRST is the bare, unqualified `jarvis`
      (Discovery.everywhereNames), and that is covered by the local-networking
      exception above rather than by the ts.net one: NSAllowsLocalNetworking
      permits cleartext to unqualified host names - names with no dot in them -
      precisely because those can only ever be resolved by something local, and
      here that something is Tailscale's MagicDNS, which installs the tailnet
      suffix as a search domain on the phone. So `jarvis` is allowed on any
      network, `jarvis.<tailnet>.ts.net` is allowed by the exception domain, and
      the bare 100.x address is allowed by neither - which is why it is last in
      the candidate list rather than first, and why nothing but the raw socket
      ever depends on it.

      Deliberately not NSAllowsArbitraryLoads, which is what most people reach
      for and which switches ATS off for the entire app.
    -->
    <key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
    <dict>
        <key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
        <true/>
        <key>NSExceptionDomains</key>
        <dict>
            <key>ts.net</key>
            <dict>
                <key>NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads</key>
                <true/>
                <key>NSIncludesSubdomains</key>
                <true/>
            </dict>
        </dict>
    </dict>

    <key>UILaunchScreen</key>
    <dict>
        <key>UIColorName</key>
        <string></string>
    </dict>

    <key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
    <dict>
        <key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
        <false/>
    </dict>

    <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
    <array>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
    </array>
    <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
    <array>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
    </array>

    <key>UIUserInterfaceStyle</key>
    <string>Dark</string>
    <key>UIRequiresFullScreen</key>
    <false/>
    <key>UIStatusBarStyle</key>
    <string>UIStatusBarStyleLightContent</string>
    <key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
    <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
